From 5009198a39412a0450f6dd02feeddea1ed622244 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: albanread <albanread@googlemail.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 16:24:12 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Win2k: a window frame after Windows 2000, and a theme with it

A new decorator after the Windows 2000 classic appearance: a raised grey
frame, two units of edge and two of face, and inside it a caption shaded
from the tab colour at its left to the border colour at its right, the
title at the left, and three raised buttons at the right -- minimize and
maximize (zoom) side by side, close set apart -- with Windows' signs, drawn
as pixel art; a pressed button sinks and its sign moves. The buttons work
on a window in the background too, as they did there. Document windows get
the ridged size grip in the corner the scroll bars leave, floating windows
a smaller caption, modal ones a dialog's frame with no caption.

The frame and the buttons are the panel colour (Windows' "3D objects"),
the caption runs from the tab colour to the border colour, active and
inactive -- Windows' title bar and its gradient -- so a theme sets every
part, and a tab and border of one colour give a flat caption. Drawn in
whole units that grow with the system font, the top border as the bar's
lower part, like Platinum.

Win2k.theme: navy captions shading to sky blue, grey ones behind, the 3D
grey for panels, menus and controls, navy selections, cream tooltips, and
the plain blue desktop with no picture.

Verified in a guest: StyledEdit and Tracker drawn active and in the
background, and a program opening one window of each look; a click on a
background window's close button closed it, minimize minimized.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
